From 546dac918f3c9d04901bd2d27667aff967f52694 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JorreFog <JorreFog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 23:06:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 05/12] Low latency (experimental, DSFLIP_LOWLAT=1, off by
 default): no frame queue, each frame at the next vblank it can make, DraStic
 held to start its frames as late as the panels allow

DraStic has no savestate API to run ahead with, so the latency taken out is the wait between a finished frame and
the panels. BananaOS runs a 2-frame queue with latch pacing: up to two refreshes in the queue plus about half a
refresh to the latch, ~40 ms. With DSFLIP_LOWLAT=1 (over DSFLIP_QUEUE, DSFLIP_QUEUE_WAIT and DSFLIP_PACING):

- a mailbox, no queue and no hold on a full one;
- a frame is committed the moment it comes while that still makes both panels' next vblank (before the cycle's
  deadline, latch_hi); one that comes after it waits for the next cycle's deadline, where a newer frame replaces it.
  Committing it at once would leave every later, on-time frame waiting behind its flip: a refresh more for good;
- DraStic is held in its present until its next frame, started then, just makes the next deadline (its time from
  a release to the next present, a decaying maximum, plus a pad that grows by 0.5 ms per missed vblank and comes
  back after a clean 10 s; with a shader its present->ready time too). It reads its buttons as it starts a frame.
  The hold lets go early while its sound runs short (the queue hold's audio_floor_us), and never holds while it
  isn't paced by its sound (its lead past 90 ms, or not known) or while its fast-forward control has toggled it on;
- the sound closer too: the pump's ring at 1024 frames, ALSA's buffer 20 ms (unless set).
DSFLIP_LOWLAT_ALIGN=0 leaves the hold out. A [lowlat] line every 10 s: how far before the deadline frames came, the
late ones, the holds and the ones cut short for the sound. Not measured on the panels yet; uneven frames (2x) show
as drops a queue would have hidden.

