Subject: [PATCH 08/12] cpugov: no strike for a frame that would be late at the
 top clock too; a low clock while the in-game menu is up; DSFLIP_CPUGOV_SUM

- A drop (or a slow window) right after a frame that would have taken more than a refresh at the top clock too
  (Mario Kart DS's race start: 150-250% of 16.7 ms) is no clock's fault: no strike, not remembered. Such strikes
  had banned 1800 MHz for Mario Kart DS, and a banned next step blocks every step down: the race stayed at 1992.
- The in-game menu (DraStic paused, the menu draws on a press, each press boosts) runs at DSFLIP_CPUGOV_MENU kHz
  (816000; 0 = off) and the game's clock comes back when it closes.
- DSFLIP_CPUGOV_SUM: the cores' worth of DraStic threads above which no step down is taken (1.2 as before).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 05beb60c0927ed2ff747d3d4db7ff28451538888)

ROCKNIXDS (agiacalone fork) conflict notes: our governor's strike rule
stays the default. The spike rule (no strike for a frame that would be
late at the top clock too) is OFF unless DSFLIP_CPUGOV_SPIKE=1, on both
the dropped and the slow paths. DSFLIP_CPUGOV_SUM keeps 1.2. The menu
clock becomes opt-in with 853021e (next).
